1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,357
Imagine you wake up tomorrow,

2
00:00:02,397 --> 00:00:05,348
and there's a hundred billion
dollars in your bank account.

3
00:00:05,879 --> 00:00:07,220
Not a hundred million.

4
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:09,287
Not one billion.

5
00:00:09,327 --> 00:00:10,641
A hundred billion.

6
00:00:11,158 --> 00:00:13,289
That's more than the entire yearly economy

7
00:00:13,329 --> 00:00:15,074
of over half the countries on Earth.

8
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,613
Your phone won't stop ringing.

9
00:00:18,653 --> 00:00:21,214
Your bank, financial advisers,

10
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:23,253
and relatives you didn't know you had.

11
00:00:23,715 --> 00:00:25,323
There's only one rule.

12
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:27,134
You have to spend all of it.

13
00:00:27,174 --> 00:00:29,538
No saving, no investing,

14
00:00:29,578 --> 00:00:32,413
and no giving it away until the very end.

15
00:00:32,982 --> 00:00:34,997
Sounds easy, right?

16
00:00:35,037 --> 00:00:36,410
It's not even close.

17
00:00:36,863 --> 00:00:39,006
Because first, you need to understand

18
00:00:39,046 --> 00:00:40,837
how much money this actually is.

19
00:00:41,365 --> 00:00:44,494
If you stacked one million
dollars in hundred dollar bills,

20
00:00:44,534 --> 00:00:46,824
the pile would be just over a meter tall.

21
00:00:47,335 --> 00:00:50,360
A billion dollars in the same
bills would rise more than

22
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:54,152
a kilometer into the sky,
taller than the Burj Khalifa.

23
00:00:54,678 --> 00:00:56,403
And a hundred billion?

24
00:00:56,443 --> 00:01:00,130
A stack around a hundred
and ten kilometers high.

25
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:03,168
Higher than the line where
space is often said to begin.

26
00:01:03,728 --> 00:01:06,088
It would weigh around a thousand tonnes.

27
00:01:07,125 --> 00:01:08,699
Laid end to end, those bills would

28
00:01:08,739 --> 00:01:10,796
stretch around the
Earth almost four times.

29
00:01:11,295 --> 00:01:13,484
And if you counted one bill every second,

30
00:01:13,524 --> 00:01:16,464
without sleeping, it would take
you about thirty-two years.

31
00:01:17,025 --> 00:01:18,900
Spend it at one dollar per second,

32
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:21,045
and you'd need more than
three thousand years.

33
00:01:21,611 --> 00:01:23,092
Or think of it this way.

34
00:01:23,132 --> 00:01:25,936
If you spent one million
dollars every single day,

35
00:01:25,976 --> 00:01:26,970
it would take you about two

36
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:28,940
hundred and seventy-four years to run out.

37
00:01:29,439 --> 00:01:30,908
The average American worker earns

38
00:01:30,948 --> 00:01:32,856
around sixty thousand dollars a year.

39
00:01:33,358 --> 00:01:35,292
To earn a hundred billion at that salary,

40
00:01:35,332 --> 00:01:38,016
you would have to work for more
than one and a half million years.

41
00:01:38,505 --> 00:01:40,656
That's longer than our
species has existed.

42
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,967
So, let's start spending.

43
00:01:43,522 --> 00:01:45,003
First, the obvious stuff.

44
00:01:45,043 --> 00:01:45,707
Cars.

45
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:47,754
A Lamborghini Aventador costs

46
00:01:47,794 --> 00:01:49,264
around half a million dollars.

47
00:01:49,304 --> 00:01:51,431
You buy ten, one in every color.

48
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:54,372
A Bugatti Chiron is about three million.

49
00:01:54,412 --> 00:01:55,367
You take five.

50
00:01:55,818 --> 00:01:58,565
And then the most expensive car ever sold.

51
00:01:58,605 --> 00:02:01,780
A 1955 Mercedes-Benz 300 SLR,

52
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:04,934
auctioned in 2022 for about a hundred
and forty-three million dollars.

53
00:02:05,899 --> 00:02:09,319
Your garage is now worth
more than most skyscrapers.

54
00:02:09,359 --> 00:02:12,078
And you've spent less than
two tenths of one percent.

55
00:02:12,593 --> 00:02:14,801
So you need somewhere to park all of this.

56
00:02:15,329 --> 00:02:19,051
In Los Angeles, a mega
mansion called The One sold

57
00:02:19,091 --> 00:02:21,717
for about a hundred and
forty-one million dollars.

58
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,929
In New York, a penthouse
overlooking Central Park

59
00:02:24,969 --> 00:02:27,108
went for around two hundred
and thirty-eight million.

60
00:02:27,639 --> 00:02:30,119
Add a private island in
the Bahamas for, let's say,

61
00:02:30,159 --> 00:02:33,018
fifty million, and you
have places to stay.

62
00:02:33,598 --> 00:02:35,485
Running total, about five hundred

63
00:02:35,525 --> 00:02:37,153
and ninety-two million dollars.

64
00:02:37,714 --> 00:02:39,214
Still not even one percent.

65
00:02:39,796 --> 00:02:41,807
Next, you need a way to get between them.

66
00:02:42,324 --> 00:02:46,089
A Gulfstream G650 private
jet, around seventy million.

67
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:48,437
But why stop there?

68
00:02:48,477 --> 00:02:51,712
A Boeing 747 turned into
a flying palace can

69
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:54,007
cost well over four
hundred million dollars.

70
00:02:54,506 --> 00:02:57,008
And for the ocean, a superyacht.

71
00:02:57,048 --> 00:02:59,587
The biggest ones cost around
half a billion dollars.

72
00:02:59,627 --> 00:03:00,546
You order one.

73
00:03:00,985 --> 00:03:02,849
And keeping a superyacht running can cost

74
00:03:02,889 --> 00:03:05,732
around ten percent of
its price every year.

75
00:03:05,772 --> 00:03:10,291
That's tens of millions, just
for crew, fuel and repairs.

76
00:03:10,831 --> 00:03:14,061
Total so far, about one point six billion.

77
00:03:14,579 --> 00:03:16,323
And your money is barely noticed.

78
00:03:16,809 --> 00:03:18,278
But you still have to live.

79
00:03:18,318 --> 00:03:20,248
So let's upgrade your daily life.

80
00:03:20,713 --> 00:03:22,275
You hire a private chef,

81
00:03:22,315 --> 00:03:25,212
a driver, and a full security team.

82
00:03:25,252 --> 00:03:27,787
That's maybe a few million dollars a year.

83
00:03:28,356 --> 00:03:32,020
You eat at the best restaurants
on the planet every single night,

84
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:34,234
at around a thousand dollars a meal.

85
00:03:34,826 --> 00:03:37,468
Do that for the next sixty years and you'd

86
00:03:37,508 --> 00:03:41,276
spend about twenty-two million dollars.

87
00:03:41,316 --> 00:03:42,027
On dinner.

88
00:03:42,663 --> 00:03:46,234
A new designer outfit every
day for the rest of your life?

89
00:03:46,274 --> 00:03:47,693
Maybe another fifty million.

90
00:03:48,445 --> 00:03:52,887
Then a personal trainer, a
doctor on call, a stylist,

91
00:03:52,927 --> 00:03:56,738
and a pilot on standby,
twenty-four hours a day.

92
00:03:57,397 --> 00:04:00,143
Every birthday, you fly in the biggest

93
00:04:00,183 --> 00:04:04,404
artists in the world to play just for you.

94
00:04:04,444 --> 00:04:07,442
That can easily cost several
million dollars a night.

95
00:04:07,927 --> 00:04:12,044
And every summer, you rent
out an entire luxury resort,

96
00:04:12,084 --> 00:04:14,246
just so nobody else is there.

97
00:04:14,684 --> 00:04:18,947
All of that luxury together
barely moves the counter.

98
00:04:19,952 --> 00:04:23,519
So let's start collecting the
rarest things money can buy.

99
00:04:24,027 --> 00:04:26,610
The most expensive wristwatch ever sold,

100
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:29,211
a Patek Philippe Grandmaster Chime,

101
00:04:29,251 --> 00:04:32,528
went for about thirty-one
million dollars in 2019.

102
00:04:33,069 --> 00:04:36,593
The Pink Star, one of the most
famous diamonds in the world,

103
00:04:36,633 --> 00:04:39,098
sold for about seventy-one million.

104
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:43,134
A 1952 Mickey Mantle baseball card sold

105
00:04:43,174 --> 00:04:45,429
for twelve point six million dollars.

106
00:04:45,937 --> 00:04:49,543
And the first Superman comic,
Action Comics number one,

107
00:04:49,583 --> 00:04:52,650
sold for six million dollars in 2024.

108
00:04:53,263 --> 00:04:54,941
You buy them all.

109
00:04:54,981 --> 00:04:57,391
Your collection is now world famous,

110
00:04:57,431 --> 00:05:00,081
and it cost you about a
hundred and twenty million.

111
00:05:00,659 --> 00:05:03,181
Then you decide to become a landlord.

112
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:05,328
The typical American home costs

113
00:05:05,368 --> 00:05:07,568
around four hundred thousand dollars.

114
00:05:07,608 --> 00:05:09,143
You buy a thousand of them.

115
00:05:09,637 --> 00:05:13,022
That's a whole town, for four
hundred million dollars.

116
00:05:13,062 --> 00:05:15,178
And you've now spent about two percent.

117
00:05:15,683 --> 00:05:17,118
Time to think bigger.

118
00:05:17,158 --> 00:05:20,098
Why buy a ticket, when you
can buy the whole team?

119
00:05:20,673 --> 00:05:23,582
In 2023, the Washington Commanders sold

120
00:05:23,622 --> 00:05:25,382
for about six billion dollars,

121
00:05:25,422 --> 00:05:27,932
a record for an American
sports team at the time.

122
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:31,243
In 2022, Chelsea Football Club was

123
00:05:31,283 --> 00:05:33,600
sold for over four billion pounds.

124
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:35,257
That's around five billion dollars.

125
00:05:35,755 --> 00:05:38,374
And in 2025, the Boston Celtics were

126
00:05:38,414 --> 00:05:40,332
sold for about six billion dollars.

127
00:05:40,842 --> 00:05:42,241
You buy all three.

128
00:05:42,281 --> 00:05:43,890
An American football team,

129
00:05:43,930 --> 00:05:47,126
an English football club,
and a basketball dynasty.

130
00:05:47,607 --> 00:05:49,908
That's another seventeen billion dollars.

131
00:05:50,378 --> 00:05:53,991
And for the first time, the
block actually looks smaller.

132
00:05:54,511 --> 00:05:56,816
What about buying a whole company?

133
00:05:56,856 --> 00:05:59,103
In 2022, Twitter was bought for

134
00:05:59,143 --> 00:06:01,004
about forty-four billion dollars.

135
00:06:01,512 --> 00:06:03,133
That would wipe out almost half

136
00:06:03,173 --> 00:06:04,801
of your fortune in a single deal.

137
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:07,899
But buying a company counts as investing.

138
00:06:07,939 --> 00:06:09,292
Rule number two.

139
00:06:09,332 --> 00:06:10,775
Transaction denied.

140
00:06:11,445 --> 00:06:13,108
Now let's buy something famous.

141
00:06:13,631 --> 00:06:14,798
The Mona Lisa?

142
00:06:14,838 --> 00:06:15,936
Not for sale.

143
00:06:15,976 --> 00:06:18,699
It belongs to France,
and French law doesn't

144
00:06:18,739 --> 00:06:21,099
allow national museum
collections to be sold.

145
00:06:21,613 --> 00:06:22,606
Okay.

146
00:06:22,646 --> 00:06:26,368
How about Leonardo's other
masterpiece, Salvator Mundi?

147
00:06:26,408 --> 00:06:29,968
It sold for four hundred and
fifty million dollars in 2017,

148
00:06:30,008 --> 00:06:31,938
the most ever paid for a painting.

149
00:06:32,556 --> 00:06:33,156
Bought.

150
00:06:33,192 --> 00:06:35,737
Even though it hasn't been
shown in public since.

151
00:06:36,211 --> 00:06:38,551
What about the tallest building on Earth?

152
00:06:38,591 --> 00:06:40,327
The Burj Khalifa cost around one

153
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:42,250
and a half billion dollars to build.

154
00:06:42,757 --> 00:06:44,377
It's not for sale either.

155
00:06:44,417 --> 00:06:47,124
But for the same money,
you could build your own.

156
00:06:47,651 --> 00:06:49,770
And in Las Vegas, the Sphere cost

157
00:06:49,810 --> 00:06:52,290
about two point three billion dollars.

158
00:06:52,330 --> 00:06:53,842
Now it shows whatever you want.

159
00:06:54,385 --> 00:06:57,222
Running total, about
twenty-three and a half billion.

160
00:06:57,737 --> 00:06:58,843
Let's go even bigger.

161
00:06:59,358 --> 00:07:02,674
The world's largest cruise
ship, Icon of the Seas,

162
00:07:02,714 --> 00:07:04,613
cost about two billion dollars,

163
00:07:04,653 --> 00:07:07,314
and can carry more than
seven thousand passengers.

164
00:07:07,847 --> 00:07:09,336
You buy it, just for your friends.

165
00:07:09,867 --> 00:07:11,080
Want your own air force?

166
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:14,517
An F-35 fighter jet costs
around eighty million dollars.

167
00:07:14,557 --> 00:07:15,453
You get twenty.

168
00:07:15,942 --> 00:07:17,620
And to carry them, the most advanced

169
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:20,581
aircraft carrier ever
built, the USS Gerald R.

170
00:07:20,621 --> 00:07:22,923
Ford, at roughly thirteen billion dollars.

171
00:07:23,428 --> 00:07:25,362
Of course, governments
don't actually sell these.

172
00:07:25,402 --> 00:07:26,635
But this is your fantasy.

173
00:07:27,148 --> 00:07:28,821
Then, space.

174
00:07:28,861 --> 00:07:31,166
A private seat on a mission
to the International

175
00:07:31,206 --> 00:07:34,344
Space Station costs around
fifty-five million dollars.

176
00:07:34,843 --> 00:07:37,655
You book one for yourself,
and ten of your friends.

177
00:07:38,175 --> 00:07:39,703
Or skip the waiting list.

178
00:07:39,743 --> 00:07:42,141
A whole SpaceX Falcon 9 launch is

179
00:07:42,181 --> 00:07:44,448
listed at around seventy million dollars.

180
00:07:44,989 --> 00:07:48,161
So you launch your own
satellite, with your name on it.

181
00:07:48,653 --> 00:07:51,989
Total spent, a little over
forty billion dollars.

182
00:07:52,528 --> 00:07:54,972
Around fifty-nine billion left.

183
00:07:55,012 --> 00:07:58,068
And this is where things get strange.

184
00:07:58,623 --> 00:08:00,394
Remember, a hundred billion

185
00:08:00,434 --> 00:08:02,619
dollars doesn't just sit there.

186
00:08:03,522 --> 00:08:07,105
Even in a simple savings
account paying four percent,

187
00:08:07,145 --> 00:08:10,770
what's left earns about two point
four billion dollars a year.

188
00:08:11,289 --> 00:08:14,322
That's about six and a half
million dollars a day.

189
00:08:14,844 --> 00:08:18,389
Or roughly seventy-five
dollars every second.

190
00:08:19,094 --> 00:08:21,980
Every minute you spend
deciding what to buy next,

191
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:24,368
you earn about four and
a half thousand dollars.

192
00:08:24,873 --> 00:08:27,619
While you were busy buying
that aircraft carrier,

193
00:08:27,659 --> 00:08:30,193
the bank was quietly paying you back.

194
00:08:30,719 --> 00:08:32,966
And it compounds.

195
00:08:33,006 --> 00:08:34,394
At four percent,

196
00:08:34,434 --> 00:08:37,478
money roughly doubles
every eighteen years.

197
00:08:38,563 --> 00:08:42,412
Leave the remaining fifty-nine
billion alone for eighteen years,

198
00:08:42,452 --> 00:08:45,590
and it grows past a hundred billion again.

199
00:08:46,202 --> 00:08:47,656
Here's another way to see it.

200
00:08:48,161 --> 00:08:51,871
If you spent ten million
dollars every single day,

201
00:08:51,911 --> 00:08:55,339
your money would still last
about twenty-six years.

202
00:08:55,847 --> 00:08:58,535
And if you only spent five million a day,

203
00:08:58,575 --> 00:09:01,159
it would never run out at all.

204
00:09:01,199 --> 00:09:03,117
The interest would keep refilling it.

205
00:09:03,608 --> 00:09:05,310
To actually run out,

206
00:09:05,350 --> 00:09:07,687
you have to spend faster
than the money grows.

207
00:09:08,251 --> 00:09:09,965
So you hire people.

208
00:09:10,005 --> 00:09:12,100
A team to run your homes,

209
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:15,371
your jets, your yacht and your teams.

210
00:09:15,938 --> 00:09:18,197
Say ten thousand employees,

211
00:09:18,237 --> 00:09:21,007
at a hundred thousand dollars a year each.

212
00:09:21,047 --> 00:09:24,718
That's a billion dollars,
every single year.

213
00:09:25,286 --> 00:09:27,394
Your stadiums, your ship and your

214
00:09:27,434 --> 00:09:30,923
carrier also cost money to run.

215
00:09:30,963 --> 00:09:33,060
Operating an aircraft carrier alone

216
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:35,460
can cost hundreds of millions per year.

217
00:09:36,047 --> 00:09:37,389
Even with all of that,

218
00:09:37,429 --> 00:09:40,403
your spending only just
keeps up with the interest.

219
00:09:40,912 --> 00:09:42,776
To truly empty the account,

220
00:09:42,816 --> 00:09:47,242
you need projects so huge that
usually only countries attempt them.

221
00:09:47,782 --> 00:09:49,933
Like building a brand new city.

222
00:09:51,049 --> 00:09:53,528
Saudi Arabia's Neom project
was announced with a

223
00:09:53,568 --> 00:09:56,299
price tag of around five
hundred billion dollars.

224
00:09:56,858 --> 00:09:58,246
Even your entire fortune would

225
00:09:58,286 --> 00:09:59,798
cover just a fraction of it.

226
00:10:00,305 --> 00:10:02,158
Or think about the Moon.

227
00:10:02,198 --> 00:10:04,491
NASA's Artemis program
is estimated to cost

228
00:10:04,531 --> 00:10:08,226
around ninety-three billion
dollars through 2025.

229
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:11,601
That's almost exactly the
fortune you started with.

230
00:10:12,155 --> 00:10:14,578
Suddenly, a hundred billion dollars

231
00:10:14,618 --> 00:10:16,281
doesn't feel infinite anymore.

232
00:10:16,779 --> 00:10:19,382
So what if you spent it on something else?

233
00:10:19,811 --> 00:10:23,149
In 2021, the head of the
UN World Food Programme

234
00:10:23,189 --> 00:10:25,750
said about six billion
dollars could help around

235
00:10:25,790 --> 00:10:28,312
forty-two million people
on the brink of famine.

236
00:10:28,765 --> 00:10:30,474
That's six percent of your money.

237
00:10:30,979 --> 00:10:32,449
A mosquito net to protect against

238
00:10:32,489 --> 00:10:34,562
malaria costs just a few dollars.

239
00:10:34,602 --> 00:10:35,862
One billion dollars could buy

240
00:10:35,902 --> 00:10:37,159
hundreds of millions of them.

241
00:10:37,656 --> 00:10:39,717
For the price of a single Lamborghini,

242
00:10:39,757 --> 00:10:42,188
you could fund dozens of
water wells for villages.

243
00:10:42,721 --> 00:10:46,094
Vaccines, clean water, schools.

244
00:10:46,134 --> 00:10:47,766
The same money that bought a stadium

245
00:10:47,806 --> 00:10:49,573
could change millions of lives.

246
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:52,644
Which is exactly what
some billionaires say

247
00:10:52,684 --> 00:10:54,708
they plan to do with most of their wealth.

248
00:10:55,192 --> 00:10:59,514
But the rule was, no giving
it away until the end.

249
00:11:00,022 --> 00:11:01,917
And here's something most people miss.

250
00:11:03,226 --> 00:11:05,009
Real billionaires almost never have a

251
00:11:05,049 --> 00:11:07,072
hundred billion dollars sitting in cash.

252
00:11:07,661 --> 00:11:08,944
Most of their wealth is shares

253
00:11:08,984 --> 00:11:10,338
in the companies they built.

254
00:11:10,378 --> 00:11:12,265
If they tried to sell everything at once,

255
00:11:12,305 --> 00:11:13,387
the price would fall.

256
00:11:13,943 --> 00:11:16,132
So they borrow against
those shares instead,

257
00:11:16,172 --> 00:11:18,590
and the shares keep
growing in the background.

258
00:11:19,124 --> 00:11:22,753
Their fortunes can rise or fall
by billions in a single day,

259
00:11:22,793 --> 00:11:24,839
just because a stock price moved.

260
00:11:25,464 --> 00:11:28,489
History has seen fortunes
like this before.

261
00:11:28,529 --> 00:11:30,602
Adjusted for inflation, John D.

262
00:11:30,642 --> 00:11:32,576
Rockefeller's wealth is often estimated

263
00:11:32,616 --> 00:11:34,511
at several hundred billion dollars.

264
00:11:35,014 --> 00:11:37,934
And Mansa Musa, the
medieval ruler of Mali,

265
00:11:37,974 --> 00:11:39,676
is said to have been so rich that

266
00:11:39,716 --> 00:11:41,797
historians can't even put a number on it.

267
00:11:42,279 --> 00:11:43,992
Which is why the richest people on Earth

268
00:11:44,032 --> 00:11:47,006
can spend wildly, and still get richer.

269
00:11:47,559 --> 00:11:49,361
So let's add everything up.

270
00:11:50,373 --> 00:11:55,592
Cars, homes, jets, a
superyacht, three sports teams,

271
00:11:55,632 --> 00:11:59,087
a painting, the Sphere, a cruise ship,

272
00:11:59,127 --> 00:12:03,390
an air force, an aircraft
carrier, and a trip to space.

273
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:05,962
A little over forty billion dollars.

274
00:12:06,496 --> 00:12:09,057
Then the staff, the running costs,

275
00:12:09,097 --> 00:12:11,562
and a lifetime of interest
working against you.

276
00:12:12,081 --> 00:12:14,398
Could you spend a hundred billion dollars?

277
00:12:14,438 --> 00:12:15,904
Technically, yes.

278
00:12:16,551 --> 00:12:18,729
But only if you go far beyond anything

279
00:12:18,769 --> 00:12:20,803
a normal person could ever imagine.

280
00:12:21,213 --> 00:12:23,378
And here's the craziest part.

281
00:12:23,418 --> 00:12:26,672
Several people on Earth are
already worth more than that.

282
00:12:27,575 --> 00:12:30,066
For them, this isn't a fantasy.

283
00:12:30,106 --> 00:12:31,455
It's just a Tuesday.

284
00:12:31,987 --> 00:12:38,032
We bought supercars,
mansions, jets, yachts, teams,

285
00:12:38,072 --> 00:12:42,091
a painting, a rocket launch
and an aircraft carrier.

286
00:12:43,064 --> 00:12:46,217
And the hardest part wasn't
finding things to buy.

287
00:12:46,257 --> 00:12:48,408
It was outrunning the money itself.

288
00:12:48,956 --> 00:12:50,553
If you had to pick just three things

289
00:12:50,593 --> 00:12:52,871
from this whole list, which would they be?

290
00:12:53,376 --> 00:12:54,857
So tell me in the comments.

291
00:12:54,897 --> 00:12:56,560
What's the first thing you would buy?

292
00:12:57,089 --> 00:13:00,091
And if you enjoyed this, subscribe.

293
00:13:00,131 --> 00:13:01,868
Next time, we'll find out what it

294
00:13:01,908 --> 00:13:03,827
really takes to become a billionaire.

